@page "/predictive-coding"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Predictive Coding";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Predictive Coding — a backprop-free, brain-inspired machine-learning research codebase in pure .NET: free-energy minimisation, a model of the visual cortex, a Thousand Brains system, a complete Transformer stack and live people counting.";
}

<header class="page-head project-head">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
        <h1>Predictive Coding</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            A .NET 10 research codebase built on one idea: the brain is a
            prediction machine, and perception and learning are free-energy
            minimisation. No backpropagation in the core, no Python — one
            hierarchical generative engine that grows into a model of the
            visual cortex, a Thousand Brains sensorimotor system, a complete
            Transformer stack and a live vision pipeline.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<p class="project-intro">
    Every layer of the stack holds a latent state, generates a top-down
    prediction of the layer below, and learns only from the gap between what
    it predicted and what it received — a rule whose every term is available
    locally, at the layer that computes it. That single locality property is
    what lets the same engine become a classifier, a memory, an agent, a
    cortex. The code and the full documentation are on
    <a href="https://github.com/matteofabbri/PredicitiveCoding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
</p>

<ol class="feature-list">
    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>Learning without backpropagation</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Local Hebbian rules</p>
            <p>
                The core network settles its hidden states by minimising free
                energy, then updates every weight from quantities available at
                that layer alone — its own prediction error and the activity
                of the layer above. No global backward pass, no autodiff
                graph, no GPU required.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>One engine, many architectures</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Composition, not special cases</p>
            <p>
                Modular hierarchies, active inference, temporal cascades,
                modern Hopfield memories, graph predictive coding, a
                mixture-of-experts gated by free energy, spiking networks —
                all built by wrapping the same core <code>Network</code>,
                without a single new learning algorithm.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>A cortex in code</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">36 brain regions</p>
            <p>
                A predictive-coding visual cortex (V1→V2→V4→IT) plus models
                of the hippocampus and grid cells, thalamus and basal
                ganglia, dopamine and noradrenaline, the amygdala, Broca and
                Wernicke — each one an assembly of already-tested
                primitives, not a new model to debug.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>A Thousand Brains system</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Hawkins / Numenta, in C#</p>
            <p>
                Cortical columns that each learn a model of an object in
                their own reference frames and vote to reach consensus — the
                published Thousand Brains science implemented paper by paper,
                with pose-invariant recognition in 2-D and 3-D and a
                sensorimotor loop that runs on real images.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>A Transformer stack, and a camera feed</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Language and vision</p>
            <p>
                A complete decoder-only Transformer — BPE tokenizer, RoPE,
                GQA, MLA, SwiGLU, KV cache, beam search, an autodiff
                trainer — and a live people counter: YOLOX detection, SORT
                tracking, re-identification, on CPU or CUDA.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>
</ol>

